VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-codex
FAMILY: crossvendor
TASK: CD4
ATTEMPT: 2
MANIFEST_SHA256: ea20b62611555fa0689133ad401a7241c0fca255de7443cc8b5d3499526fac1e
CODEX_MODEL: gpt-6-sol
CODEX_VERSION: codex-cli 0.159.0
---
criterion b: PASS
    attack: Run every gate sub-suite in the data-free copy.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_tests.sh | grep -E '^(==|FAIL|ALL PASS|[0-9]+ FAILED)'
    result: run_check.sh, run_escalate.sh, run_done.sh, and doc_test.sh reported ALL PASS. agents_test.sh and verify_layout.sh failed only on omitted .claude/agents files and .env.example.
criterion c: PASS
    attack: Run the complete dashboard suite.
    command: node --test dashboard/test/*.test.mjs
    result: 126 tests passed; 0 failed.
criterion d.1: PASS
    attack: Give checker-codex an anthropic FAMILY, including at tiers 1–3.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_check.sh; node --test dashboard/test/*.test.mjs
    result: Both suites rejected the invalid pairing. Independent tier 1–3 fixtures also produced gate refusal and dashboard blocked state.
criterion d.2: PASS
    attack: Use codex as the only ledger check.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_check.sh; node --test dashboard/test/*.test.mjs
    result: The suites refused codex-only rows. Independent fixtures refused them at all three tiers.
criterion d.3: PASS
    attack: Try a skip, both records together, NUL bytes, stale evidence, and locale-sensitive whitespace.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_check.sh; node --test dashboard/test/*.test.mjs
    result: The suites accepted valid current-attempt skips and refused both records, missing evidence, and NUL-bearing skips. The gate tests produced the same skip-header decisions under C and C.utf8.
criterion d.4: PASS
    attack: Try to use a Codex PASS as the tier 2 or tier 3 second lane, and omit its MANIFEST_SHA256.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_check.sh | grep -E 'codex: (tier 3: a Codex PASS|tier 2: a Codex PASS|a Codex PASS without MANIFEST_SHA256)'
    result: The suite reported passing assertions that the PASS supplied no second lane or named checker and that the missing fingerprint header was refused.
criterion d.5: PASS
    attack: Use named and unnamed Codex FAILs, including consecutive failures and judge disputes.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_check.sh; node --test dashboard/test/*.test.mjs
    result: The suites reported tier 1 named refusal, tier 2/3 disputes, consecutive-fail escalation, and stats counting.
criterion d.6: PASS
    attack: Place Codex verdicts on rows that do not name codex.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_check.sh
    result: Tier 1 acceptance ignored an unnamed Codex FAIL; tier 2/3 tests opened disputes for one. Unnamed skips did not supply a lane.
criterion d.7: PASS
    attack: Count attempt 0 and invalid checker-second evidence in stats.
    command: bash smoketest/gate/run_check.sh
    result: The suite reported the attempt-0 pair, current-attempt suffixes, and second=none for an invalid checker-second. Mutating either count to start at 1 or reading the invalid second directly made the suite fail.
criterion d.8: PASS
    attack: Put a dangling Codex verdict symlink beside a valid tier 1 skip.
    command: bash - <<'SH'
        source smoketest/gate/_lib.sh
        sd=$(newswarm)
        mkledger "$sd" 'x\t1\ttests,codex\taccepted\t1\tw\tr\n'
        mkverdict "$sd" x 1 checker-tests PASS anthropic
        mkskip "$sd" x 1 quota
        ln -s nowhere "$sd/verdicts/x.1.checker-codex.verdict"
        for action in check done; do out=$(gate_out "$sd" "$action" x); rc=$?; echo "$action rc=$rc :: $(grep -E '^(FAIL:)' <<<"$out" | tr '\n' ' ')"; done
        SH
    result: Both check and done exited 1 with invalid verdict checker-codex. The dashboard suite passed its non-regular-entry cases.
criterion d.9: PASS
    attack: Compare check and done on valid and invalid Codex evidence.
    command: bash - <<'SH'
        source smoketest/gate/_lib.sh
        sd=$(newswarm)
        mkledger "$sd" 'x\t1\ttests,codex\taccepted\t1\tw\tr\n'
        mkverdict "$sd" x 1 checker-tests PASS anthropic
        mkskip "$sd" x 1 quota
        for action in check done; do gate_out "$sd" "$action" x; echo "rc=$?"; done
        ln -s nowhere "$sd/verdicts/x.1.checker-codex.verdict"
        for action in check done; do gate_out "$sd" "$action" x; echo "rc=$?"; done
        SH
    result: Both commands accepted the valid skip, then both refused the invalid verdict entry.
criterion f: PASS
    attack: Run the documentation pins and inspect Codex, FAMILY, and critical-glob statements.
    command: bash smoketest/doc_test.sh; grep -nE 'codex|crossvendor|critical-glob-unreadable' CLAUDE.md TIERS.md README.md | head -90
    result: doc_test.sh reported ALL PASS. The inspected passages describe the trial, lead ownership, valid FAMILY pairing, fail-closed glob rule, and unnamed tier 1 FAIL escalation and stats.
criterion i: FAIL
    attack: In an isolated scratch copy, remove parse.mjs’s pre-open regular-file guard while retaining its nonblocking open and post-open fstat check. This mutation opens a FIFO verdict entry.
    command: node --test /work/_adversarial_mutant/dashboard/test/*.test.mjs | tail -12
    result: The mutated dashboard suite still reported 126 passed and 0 failed. Instrumenting fs.openSync on a FIFO verdict fixture recorded FIFO openSync calls=1. The shipped tests therefore do not catch this specified regression. The other five specified mutations each made a shipped suite fail in separate scratch copies.
observations:
    - The wrapper failures on .claude/agents/** and .env.example are copy artefacts: those paths are explicitly omitted.
    - With a changed tree file after fingerprinting, check refused for tree drift while done accepted because done skips the live tree rehash. This is existing fingerprint behavior outside the Codex evidence change.
